Hidden class i has prior mass prior_counts[i]. The evidence likelihood is likelihood[i]/10. Class i calls for action i. Compute the posterior distribution over required actions.

The university’s course enrollment office schedules section assignments each term. To keep the system manageable, the office uses a fixed number of action categories, and each student’s final schedule falls into exactly one of those categories. The assignment process starts from an initial index that depends on the student’s program requirements, and then a scheduling override may shift the index. The override is applied by an automated policy that adds a small adjustment to the initial index and wraps around modulo the number of action categories. The office does not observe which override case occurs; it only knows that one of several possible latent cases is chosen each term with a likelihood proportional to a weight. These weights reflect historical frequencies from past terms, but the office does not record which specific case produced any individual student’s schedule. The final outcome index is simply the result of the policy applied to the chosen latent case. The office is preparing a summary for an advising committee. The committee wants to understand how often each possible final outcome index would occur under this process, given the known list of latent cases and their weights. No other information is available to the office, and no additional assumptions should be made about the latent cases. The summary must be based solely on the provided facts and the stated rule.

